          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1039 belt: the LABEL-anchored half of the subpremise mask — an `Apt/Suite` label node followed by its value, whatever spelling that value carries (`Unit 4202` shipped raw 2026-08-14). Both label spellings are enumerated because `hasPrecedingSiblingText` is an exact, un-trimmed equality; whole-node `plainMask` because a unit number is a bounded ~10^4 alphabet. Ordered ahead of every generic shape entry in this block: `maskNode` is first-match-wins, and a digit-leading value (`101 B`) would otherwise be claimed and HASHED by the id-less street shape. See `dropoff_handoff` for the full reasoning. Inert without the label; pinned by the #1039 parity test.",
          "find": {
            "any": [
              {
                "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Apt/Suite"
              },
              {
                "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Apt/Suite:"
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "The id-less first-name + last-initial customer name. Whole-value anchored (^...$, whitespace-tolerant so a trailing space still masks) so it masks ONLY the bare name node, never the multi-word merchant anchor, the zone code, a count, or the 'Confirm ...'/'Mix-ups ...' copy. Char classes cover accented/Unicode letters (José, Muñoz), interior capitals (McKenna), hyphenated first names (Mary-Jo), and apostrophes (O'Brien, D'Angelo); every quantifier is bounded ({0,20}/{0,3}/{0,8}/{1,4}) so RegexSafety's ReDoS guard accepts it. Compiled IGNORE_CASE (all rule regexes are), so 'JOSE G' masks too. SSOT with SnapshotRedactor.FIRST_LAST_INITIAL_PATTERN. #885: the token SEPARATOR is `\\s{1,4}`, not a literal space — the 07-26 field render was 'Firstname  L.' with a DOUBLE space, which the old single-space separator did not match, so this entry existed but never fired and a raw customer name reached the recognized envelope. `normalize: customerName` (#733) canonicalizes the token before the <4hex> distinctness hash, so this card's mask hex equals the same customer's mask on 'Deliver to <name>' surfaces AND the persisted customerNameHash — one customer, one mask, across every surface form.",
