        {
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "address_line_2"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#934: plainMask — the id-anchored member of the #895/#892 family, and the original one the issue was filed on. Fielded 2026-08-01 on three `dropoff_pre_arrival` envelopes as 'Apt/Suite: [redacted:<4hex>]' (real fielded hex withheld — it is the brute-recoverable token this entry removes). Note this SAME rule fielded the plain form ('Apt [redacted]', 08-02) from its fused `Apt ` entry below — one surface, two spellings, and only one of them was covered, which is exactly why this is a family fix and not a one-entry fix. The masked token after the label strip is a bare unit number: bounded ~10^4, above the #889 length floor at 4 digits, brute-recoverable from the 4-hex suffix. Distinctness is redundant (`user_name`/`address_line_*` on this same surface keep per-customer hashes) and a unit number joins nothing. Capture-only — the node is never parsed, so the goldens are unchanged.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "address_subpremise_line"
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Apt/Suite: ",
            "Apt/Suite "
          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#860: the address block's 'Building Name' VALUE — an apartment-complex or building label (free-form proper-noun text, sometimes a bare building number) is customer-locating PII, and it was the ONE labeled field in this block shipping raw: street/city/zip fall to the id-less numeric shapes below, 'Entry code'/'Apt/Suite' values to the `^#?\\s?\\d{1,4}[A-Za-z]?$` entry, the instruction body to the dasher_instruction_* ids — but a building name has no id, no digits, and no stable text shape, so nothing named it. Anchored on the LABEL sibling (app chrome, not customer data), which is the only tight handle; the label node itself is NOT redacted (a label is vocabulary, not PII). Hash-mask family (no plainMask): a building name is a large, unbounded space, so the <4hex> distinctness suffix is not brute-recoverable and it buys per-complex replay fidelity. No `normalize` — that canonicalizer is the customer-NAME key and would degrade an address-class token's mask distinctness.",
          "find": {
            "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Building Name"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1058 review (Astra xhigh) \u2014 `plainMask`, the #920/#985 posture. The node is the customer's own delivery-instruction body, and its ALPHABET is unbounded but its LENGTH is not: a SHORT instruction that IS the secret (\"Hand it to me: 4417\") masks whole to `[redacted:<4hex>]`, and enumerating `Hand it to me: 0000`\u2026`9999` against those 65 536 buckets recovers the code \u2014 the #889 F1 bounded-secret argument, arriving through length rather than through alphabet. The distinctness the hash buys is worth nothing here to trade against that: unlike a customer NAME (which `normalize: customerName` turns into a cross-surface join key) an instruction body joins nothing and is never parsed. Pre-existing on this rule; #1058's fourth `require` arm is what EXPOSED it, by making the alcohol render \u2014 whose instruction body carried a door code \u2014 a recognized frame this entry actually fires on. Both states of the field carry the same declaration, and `plainMask` is legal here precisely because there is no `normalize` (the compiler rejects the pair).",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "dasher_instruction_content_collapsed"
          },
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1058 review: `plainMask`, for the reason spelled out on the collapsed twin directly above \u2014 same customer text, same bounded-length secret, same #920/#985 posture. Enumerating only the state that happened to field is the debt #986 already paid for once.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "dasher_instruction_content_expanded"
          },
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1039 belt (the #993 combined-frame precedent): the FUSED subpremise line — `Apt/Suite <n>` or `Apt/Suite: <n>` in ONE id-less node, the render the committed `dropoff_pre_arrival` fixtures carry. Declared on EVERY rule in this file (plus `navigation_generic`) rather than only on the surfaces that fielded it, because a redact protects only the frames its OWN rule wins and the rules most likely to claim an address-block frame OUT-RANK the fielded pair (`delivery_summary_collapsed` 31, `dropoff_navigation` 61, `dropoff_photo` 62, `dropoff_geofence_warning` 84). Entry is `dropoff_photo`'s #986 entry verbatim — see it for the full reasoning: the token after the label strip is a bounded ~10^4 unit number, so a 4-hex distinctness suffix is brute-recoverable (`plainMask`), while the label itself is app vocabulary and is kept for replay context. ACCEPTED COLLATERAL: the split row's BARE `Apt/Suite` LABEL node is itself an exact prefix match here, and no keepPrefix can be kept from it, so it masks whole to plain `[redacted]` — two words of app chrome lost on a debug envelope, the same fail-toward-privacy trade `DropoffBannerRedactParityTest` already documents for the `Arriving at` label. It cannot break the label-sibling entry below: redact predicates are evaluated against the ORIGINAL tree, the mask lands on a copy. Inert on any frame without the line; the #1039 parity test in `CaptureRedactionCorpusTest` pins that every rule declares it, so a new dropoff surface cannot forget.",
          "find": {
            "hasTextStartsWith": "Apt/Suite"
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Apt/Suite: ",
            "Apt/Suite "
          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1039 belt: the LABEL-anchored half of the subpremise mask — an `Apt/Suite` label node followed by its value, whatever spelling that value carries (`Unit 4202` shipped raw 2026-08-14). Both label spellings are enumerated because `hasPrecedingSiblingText` is an exact, un-trimmed equality; whole-node `plainMask` because a unit number is a bounded ~10^4 alphabet. Ordered ahead of every generic shape entry in this block: `maskNode` is first-match-wins, and a digit-leading value (`101 B`) would otherwise be claimed and HASHED by the id-less street shape. See `dropoff_handoff` for the full reasoning. Inert without the label; pinned by the #1039 parity test.",
          "find": {
            "any": [
              {
                "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Apt/Suite"
              },
              {
                "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Apt/Suite:"
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^\\d{1,5}\\s+\\S"
              },
              {
                "hasNoId": true
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#886 (venue variant): this card's address block is id-LESS and label-less — `<line 1><line 2>` — so line 1 is named only by shape. A street line falls to the `^\\d{1,5}\\s+\\S` entry above, but when the drop-off destination is a BUSINESS/VENUE line 1 is the venue NAME ('Sample Family Medical and Urgent Care', fielded 07-26): no id, no digits, no stable shape, and no preceding sibling to anchor on (it is child 0) — so it shipped RAW while its own city/ST/ZIP sibling masked correctly via the `\\d{5}` entry. That is a customer-LOCATION leak on a recognized surface. Anchor is the FOLLOWING sibling (#886's mirror predicate): the city/ST/ZIP line is app-rendered address chrome with a stable two-letter-state + 5-digit-ZIP shape, the only tight handle for a child-0 node. It covers BOTH forms of line 1 (venue AND street), so it is also defense-in-depth on the numeric entry. Masked whole, un-normalized (that canonicalizer is the customer-NAME key; an address/venue token would lose mask distinctness). Over-match surface is exactly 'the node directly above a city/ST/ZIP line' — in this card, only the address block's line 1.",
          "find": {
            "hasFollowingSiblingTextMatchesRegex": "[,\\s][A-Za-z]{2},?\\s{1,4}\\d{5}"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#889 F1: plainMask. The realistic population of this whole-node match is the city/state/ZIP line and bare 5-digit codes — a street line is claimed by the earlier '^\\d{1,5}\\s+\\S' entry and a gate/PIN body by the keyword entries above, both of which keep their hash. 10^5 values over 65 536 buckets is ~1.5 candidates per bucket, i.e. effectively recoverable, and the distinctness it would buy here is redundant: the street-address and name entries on this same surface already carry per-customer distinct masks. Fail toward privacy on a bounded token whose distinctness is redundant. Trade accepted: a long free-text body that happens to contain a 5-digit run also plains (it is masked WHOLE either way; its identity is not a join key).",
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasTextMatchesRegex": "\\d{5}"
              },
              {
                "hasNoId": true
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#895 (+ the 07-29 F4 field finding): plainMask. The fused 'Apt <n>' unit number is a bounded ~10^4 alphabet over the 4-char length floor, so a 4-hex distinctness suffix is brute-recoverable (the #892/#795 oracle argument); distinctness is redundant here — the same surface's name/street entries keep per-customer hashes. The split label/value form plains via the id-less digit-shape entry when its VALUE is bare digits, and via the `Apt/Suite` label-sibling entry ABOVE when it is not (#1039 — that entry is ordered ahead of every generic shape entry deliberately, since `maskNode` is first-match-wins).",
          "find": {
            "hasTextStartsWith": "Apt "
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Apt "
          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasTextStartsWith": "\""
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#889 F1: plainMask. This id-less digit shape matches an Apt/Suite/entry-code VALUE AND the PIN keypad's digit echoes — the FIELDED #889 envelope was a dropoff_pin_entry frame carrying a single-glyph keypad echo, and pin_entry (priority 63) WINS over dropoff_pin_keypad (priority 109), whose own digit entry already declares plainMask. The #889 engine length floor plains 1-3 char tokens, but a COMPLETED 4-digit PIN clears it while spanning only 10^4 values against the 65 536 4-hex buckets (~85% injective), so the distinctness suffix would be brute-recoverable. plainMask drops it — the same #795 posture as the sibling keypad rule. The floor bounds LENGTH, this declaration bounds ALPHABET; they compose. Capture-only: these nodes are never parsed, goldens unchanged.",
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^#?\\s?\\d{1,4}[A-Za-z]?$"
              },
              {
                "hasNoId": true
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        }
      ],
      "state": {
        "flow": "task:dropoff:navigation",
        "modeHint": "online"
      },
      "comment": "Dropoff layouts (#462/#460/#549): (1) the en-route nav card ('Deliver to <name>' + Directions/Call/Message); (2) the arrival detail card with 'Delivery for' + 'Hand it to recipient'; (3) #549: the arrival detail card whose instruction is NOT 'Hand it to recipient' (e.g. 'Leave it at the door'), anchored on 'Delivery for' + the complete_delivery_steps_button id (the third require branch) — it was dropping to UNKNOWN. The arrival card's first text is 'Delivery for'/'Deliver by', neither of which startsWith 'Deliver to'. 'Hand it to recipient' / complete_delivery_steps_button are the dropoff-specific discriminators (the pickup card also carries 'Delivery for', so keying on that alone would steal it). The arrival card's address nodes carry ids (address_line_1/_2), so customerAddressHash uses a coalesce (id-less shape first, by-id second). The alcohol variant additionally carries the priority-0 sensitive id/signature banner (#463), blocked before reaching here. Recognize-only (flow stays task:dropoff:navigation); the gate-code/instruction node is never parsed. (4) #1058, fielded 08-28 (4 envelopes): the ALCOHOL arrival card renders `alcohol_dropoff_instructions_title` INSTEAD of any customer lead-in — no `Deliver to` / `Heading to` / `Delivery for` node exists on it — so all three arms above missed and the sheet fell UNKNOWN, shipping its `address_subpremise_line` and its `dasher_instruction_content_collapsed` (a customer note carrying a door code) verbatim on the UNKNOWN capture path. The fourth arm is purely ID-anchored (#938: view ids do not localize): the drop-off bottom sheet container + the alcohol scan icon + the Directions button, three ids that co-occur only on this card (two committed fixtures carry all three, both already `dropoff_pre_arrival`). Recognition alone closes the leak — this rule's `redact` already declares both leaked ids — and the parse's name/address fields are nullable coalesces that simply resolve null on this render, so nothing is fabricated.",
      "require": {
        "any": [
          {
            "all": [
              {
                "exists": {
                  "any": [
                    {
                      "hasTextStartsWith": "Deliver to"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasTextStartsWith": "Heading to"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              },
              {
                "exists": {
                  "any": [
                    {
                      "hasText": "Directions"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasText": "Call"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasText": "Message"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "all": [
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Delivery for"
                }
              },
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasText": "Hand it to recipient"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "all": [
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Delivery for"
                }
              },
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "complete_delivery_steps_button"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "all": [
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "drop_off_bottom_sheet_container"
                }
              },
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "alcohol_dropoff_ic_scan"
                }
              },
              {
                "exists": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "directions_button"
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "parse": {
        "as": "task",
        "fields": {
          "phase": "DROPOFF",
          "subFlow": "NAVIGATION",
          "customerNameHash": {
            "coalesce": [
              {
                "find": {
                  "any": [
                    {
                      "hasTextStartsWith": "Deliver to"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasTextStartsWith": "Heading to"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "read": "text",
                "transform": [
                  {
                    "stripPrefixes": [
                      "Deliver to ",
                      "Heading to "
                    ]
                  },
                  "trim",
                  "normalizeCustomerName",
                  "sha256"
                ]
              },
              {
                "find": {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Delivery for"
                },
                "navigate": "sibling(1)",
                "read": "text",
                "transform": [
                  "trim",
                  "normalizeCustomerName",
                  "sha256"
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "customerAddressHash": {
            "comment": "#549: two address layouts. The en-route nav card's address is id-LESS (matched by the digit-leading shape, first branch). The arrival detail card's address carries ids (address_line_1/_2), so the id-less branch returns null and the second by-id branch reads it. coalesce picks the first non-null and applies NO top-level transform, so EACH branch hashes its own result — the address is never stored in the clear.",
            "coalesce": [
              {
                "join": [
                  {
                    "find": {
                      "all": [
                        {
                          "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^\\d{1,5}\\s+\\S"
                        },
                        {
                          "hasNoId": true
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    "read": "text"
                  },
                  {
                    "find": {
                      "all": [
                        {
                          "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^\\d{1,5}\\s+\\S"
                        },
                        {
                          "hasNoId": true
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    "navigate": "sibling(1)",
                    "rejectIf": {
                      "not": {
                        "hasTextMatchesRegex": "\\d{5}$"
                      }
                    },
                    "read": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "separator": ", ",
                "transform": "sha256"
              },
              {
                "join": [
                  {
                    "find": {
                      "hasIdSuffix": "address_line_1"
                    },
                    "read": "text"
                  },
                  {
                    "find": {
                      "hasIdSuffix": "address_line_2"
                    },
                    "read": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "separator": ", ",
                "transform": "sha256"
              }
            ]
          },
          "storeName": {
            "comment": "#526: the dropoff card's merchant label — the same store as the pickup, but in DoorDash's running-key form ('Target (02426)', 'Maple Street Biscuit - Alamo Ranch'), which does NOT string-match the offer/pickup name. The node has no id, so it's anchored by text shape: '<name> (<storeNum>)' (reliable for chains) first, then a strict all-Title-Case '<Name> - <Area>' (so a lowercase delivery instruction can't false-match). Not PII (a merchant name); feeds per-task store self-labelling + the post-session store-entity projector (#159). Null when the card variant doesn't show it.",
            "coalesce": [
              {
                "find": {
                  "all": [
                    {
                      "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^.{1,60} \\((\\d{2,6}(-\\d{1,6})?|[A-Z][A-Za-z .'&-]+)\\)$"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasNoId": true
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "read": "text",
                "transform": "trim"
              },
              {
                "find": {
                  "all": [
                    {
                      "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^[A-Z][\\w .'&-]{1,48} - [A-Z][\\w .'&-]{1,48}$"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasNoId": true
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "read": "text",
                "transform": "trim"
              }
            ]
          },
          "deadlineText": {
            "find": {
              "hasTextMatchesRegex": "\\bby\\s+\\d{1,2}:\\d{2}"
            },
            "read": "text",
            "transform": "stripDeadlinePrefix"
          },
          "deadlineMillis": {
            "find": {
              "hasTextMatchesRegex": "\\bby\\s+\\d{1,2}:\\d{2}"
            },
            "read": "text",
            "transform": "parseDeadline"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.dropoff_pre_arrival_completion",
      "priority": 74,
      "redact": [
        {
          "comment": "#993 belt (the FIX 3 precedent): this dropoff-phase surface can win a frame that STILL carries DoorDash's nav arrival banner — `dropoff_navigation`'s own reject (Continue / Complete Delivery / Mark as delivered / complete_delivery_steps_button) hands the arrival-MOMENT frame to whichever arrived-phase rule matches, and nav_arriving rejects bottom-sheet frames, so neither of the two rules that already masked `arriving_at_title` covers it. The node is id-BEARING, so the id-less name/address entries elsewhere in this block structurally cannot reach it (`hasNoId` conjunct). Whole token, no normalize — it is a customer ADDRESS on every dropoff-phase surface (see dropoff_navigation for the full reasoning). Inert on any frame without the node; `DropoffBannerRedactParityTest` pins that EVERY rule in this file carries it, so a new dropoff surface cannot forget.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "arriving_at_title"
          }
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "any": [
              {
                "hasTextStartsWith": "Deliver to"
              },
              {
                "hasTextStartsWith": "Heading to"
              }
            ]
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Deliver to ",
            "Heading to "
          ],
          "normalize": "customerName"
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1039 belt (the #993 combined-frame precedent): the FUSED subpremise line — `Apt/Suite <n>` or `Apt/Suite: <n>` in ONE id-less node, the render the committed `dropoff_pre_arrival` fixtures carry. Declared on EVERY rule in this file (plus `navigation_generic`) rather than only on the surfaces that fielded it, because a redact protects only the frames its OWN rule wins and the rules most likely to claim an address-block frame OUT-RANK the fielded pair (`delivery_summary_collapsed` 31, `dropoff_navigation` 61, `dropoff_photo` 62, `dropoff_geofence_warning` 84). Entry is `dropoff_photo`'s #986 entry verbatim — see it for the full reasoning: the token after the label strip is a bounded ~10^4 unit number, so a 4-hex distinctness suffix is brute-recoverable (`plainMask`), while the label itself is app vocabulary and is kept for replay context. ACCEPTED COLLATERAL: the split row's BARE `Apt/Suite` LABEL node is itself an exact prefix match here, and no keepPrefix can be kept from it, so it masks whole to plain `[redacted]` — two words of app chrome lost on a debug envelope, the same fail-toward-privacy trade `DropoffBannerRedactParityTest` already documents for the `Arriving at` label. It cannot break the label-sibling entry below: redact predicates are evaluated against the ORIGINAL tree, the mask lands on a copy. Inert on any frame without the line; the #1039 parity test in `CaptureRedactionCorpusTest` pins that every rule declares it, so a new dropoff surface cannot forget.",
          "find": {
            "hasTextStartsWith": "Apt/Suite"
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Apt/Suite: ",
            "Apt/Suite "
          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1039 belt: the LABEL-anchored half of the subpremise mask — an `Apt/Suite` label node followed by its value, whatever spelling that value carries (`Unit 4202` shipped raw 2026-08-14). Both label spellings are enumerated because `hasPrecedingSiblingText` is an exact, un-trimmed equality; whole-node `plainMask` because a unit number is a bounded ~10^4 alphabet. Ordered ahead of every generic shape entry in this block: `maskNode` is first-match-wins, and a digit-leading value (`101 B`) would otherwise be claimed and HASHED by the id-less street shape. See `dropoff_handoff` for the full reasoning. Inert without the label; pinned by the #1039 parity test.",
          "find": {
            "any": [
              {
                "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Apt/Suite"
              },
              {
                "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Apt/Suite:"
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^\\d{1,5}\\s+\\S"
              },
              {
                "hasNoId": true
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#886 (venue variant): this card's address block is id-LESS and label-less — `<line 1><line 2>` — so line 1 is named only by shape. A street line falls to the `^\\d{1,5}\\s+\\S` entry above, but when the drop-off destination is a BUSINESS/VENUE line 1 is the venue NAME ('Sample Family Medical and Urgent Care', fielded 07-26): no id, no digits, no stable shape, and no preceding sibling to anchor on (it is child 0) — so it shipped RAW while its own city/ST/ZIP sibling masked correctly via the `\\d{5}` entry. That is a customer-LOCATION leak on a recognized surface. Anchor is the FOLLOWING sibling (#886's mirror predicate): the city/ST/ZIP line is app-rendered address chrome with a stable two-letter-state + 5-digit-ZIP shape, the only tight handle for a child-0 node. It covers BOTH forms of line 1 (venue AND street), so it is also defense-in-depth on the numeric entry. Masked whole, un-normalized (that canonicalizer is the customer-NAME key; an address/venue token would lose mask distinctness). Over-match surface is exactly 'the node directly above a city/ST/ZIP line' — in this card, only the address block's line 1.",
          "find": {
            "hasFollowingSiblingTextMatchesRegex": "[,\\s][A-Za-z]{2},?\\s{1,4}\\d{5}"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#889 F1: plainMask. The realistic population of this whole-node match is the city/state/ZIP line and bare 5-digit codes — a street line is claimed by the earlier '^\\d{1,5}\\s+\\S' entry and a gate/PIN body by the keyword entries above, both of which keep their hash. 10^5 values over 65 536 buckets is ~1.5 candidates per bucket, i.e. effectively recoverable, and the distinctness it would buy here is redundant: the street-address and name entries on this same surface already carry per-customer distinct masks. Fail toward privacy on a bounded token whose distinctness is redundant. Trade accepted: a long free-text body that happens to contain a 5-digit run also plains (it is masked WHOLE either way; its identity is not a join key).",
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasTextMatchesRegex": "\\d{5}"
              },
              {
                "hasNoId": true
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#895 (+ the 07-29 F4 field finding): plainMask. The fused 'Apt <n>' unit number is a bounded ~10^4 alphabet over the 4-char length floor, so a 4-hex distinctness suffix is brute-recoverable (the #892/#795 oracle argument); distinctness is redundant here — the same surface's name/street entries keep per-customer hashes. The split label/value form plains via the id-less digit-shape entry when its VALUE is bare digits, and via the `Apt/Suite` label-sibling entry ABOVE when it is not (#1039 — that entry is ordered ahead of every generic shape entry deliberately, since `maskNode` is first-match-wins).",
          "find": {
            "hasTextStartsWith": "Apt "
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Apt "
          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasTextStartsWith": "\""
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#889 F1: plainMask. This id-less digit shape matches an Apt/Suite/entry-code VALUE AND the PIN keypad's digit echoes — the FIELDED #889 envelope was a dropoff_pin_entry frame carrying a single-glyph keypad echo, and pin_entry (priority 63) WINS over dropoff_pin_keypad (priority 109), whose own digit entry already declares plainMask. The #889 engine length floor plains 1-3 char tokens, but a COMPLETED 4-digit PIN clears it while spanning only 10^4 values against the 65 536 4-hex buckets (~85% injective), so the distinctness suffix would be brute-recoverable. plainMask drops it — the same #795 posture as the sibling keypad rule. The floor bounds LENGTH, this declaration bounds ALPHABET; they compose. Capture-only: these nodes are never parsed, goldens unchanged.",
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^#?\\s?\\d{1,4}[A-Za-z]?$"
              },
              {
                "hasNoId": true
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#860: same address-block 'Building Name' VALUE as dropoff_pre_arrival — this rule renders the SAME workflow sheet one CTA-state later and carries the same id-less label/value rows, so it inherits the same gap: free-form proper-noun text the digit-shape entries above structurally cannot reach. Predicate and rationale identical to the pre-arrival entry (label-anchored, hash-mask, no plainMask/normalize). Inert when the row is absent — over-redaction is the only failure direction.",
          "find": {
            "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Building Name"
          }
        }
      ],
      "state": {
        "flow": "task:dropoff:navigation",
        "modeHint": "online"
      },
      "require": {
        "all": [
          {
            "exists": {
              "any": [
                {
                  "hasTextStartsWith": "Deliver to"
                },
                {
                  "hasTextStartsWith": "Heading to"
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "exists": {
              "any": [
                {
                  "hasText": "Continue"
                },
                {
                  "hasText": "Complete Delivery"
                },
                {
                  "hasText": "Mark as delivered"
                },
                {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "complete_delivery_steps_button"
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      "parse": {
        "as": "task",
        "fields": {
          "phase": "DROPOFF",
          "subFlow": "NAVIGATION",
          "customerNameHash": {
            "find": {
              "any": [
                {
                  "hasTextStartsWith": "Deliver to"
                },
                {
                  "hasTextStartsWith": "Heading to"
                }
              ]
            },
            "read": "text",
            "transform": [
              {
                "stripPrefixes": [
                  "Deliver to ",
                  "Heading to "
                ]
              },
              "trim",
              "normalizeCustomerName",
              "sha256"
            ]
          },
          "customerAddressHash": {
            "join": [
              {
                "find": {
                  "all": [
                    {
                      "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^\\d{1,5}\\s+\\S"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasNoId": true
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "read": "text"
              },
              {
                "find": {
                  "all": [
                    {
                      "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^\\d{1,5}\\s+\\S"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasNoId": true
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "navigate": "sibling(1)",
                "rejectIf": {
                  "not": {
                    "hasTextMatchesRegex": "\\d{5}$"
                  }
                },
                "read": "text"
              }
            ],
            "separator": ", ",
            "transform": "sha256"
          },
          "deadlineText": {
            "find": {
              "hasTextMatchesRegex": "\\bby\\s+\\d{1,2}:\\d{2}"
            },
            "read": "text",
            "transform": "stripDeadlinePrefix"
          },
          "deadlineMillis": {
            "find": {
              "hasTextMatchesRegex": "\\bby\\s+\\d{1,2}:\\d{2}"
            },
            "read": "text",
            "transform": "parseDeadline"
          }
        }
      }
    },
