    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.delivery_summary_expanded",
      "priority": 30,
      "redact": [
        {
          "comment": "#993 belt (the FIX 3 precedent): this dropoff-phase surface can win a frame that STILL carries DoorDash's nav arrival banner — `dropoff_navigation`'s own reject (Continue / Complete Delivery / Mark as delivered / complete_delivery_steps_button) hands the arrival-MOMENT frame to whichever arrived-phase rule matches, and nav_arriving rejects bottom-sheet frames, so neither of the two rules that already masked `arriving_at_title` covers it. The node is id-BEARING, so the id-less name/address entries elsewhere in this block structurally cannot reach it (`hasNoId` conjunct). Whole token, no normalize — it is a customer ADDRESS on every dropoff-phase surface (see dropoff_navigation for the full reasoning). Inert on any frame without the node; `DropoffBannerRedactParityTest` pins that EVERY rule in this file carries it, so a new dropoff surface cannot forget.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "arriving_at_title"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1039 belt (the #993 combined-frame precedent): the FUSED subpremise line — `Apt/Suite <n>` or `Apt/Suite: <n>` in ONE id-less node, the render the committed `dropoff_pre_arrival` fixtures carry. Declared on EVERY rule in this file (plus `navigation_generic`) rather than only on the surfaces that fielded it, because a redact protects only the frames its OWN rule wins and the rules most likely to claim an address-block frame OUT-RANK the fielded pair (`delivery_summary_collapsed` 31, `dropoff_navigation` 61, `dropoff_photo` 62, `dropoff_geofence_warning` 84). Entry is `dropoff_photo`'s #986 entry verbatim — see it for the full reasoning: the token after the label strip is a bounded ~10^4 unit number, so a 4-hex distinctness suffix is brute-recoverable (`plainMask`), while the label itself is app vocabulary and is kept for replay context. ACCEPTED COLLATERAL: the split row's BARE `Apt/Suite` LABEL node is itself an exact prefix match here, and no keepPrefix can be kept from it, so it masks whole to plain `[redacted]` — two words of app chrome lost on a debug envelope, the same fail-toward-privacy trade `DropoffBannerRedactParityTest` already documents for the `Arriving at` label. It cannot break the label-sibling entry below: redact predicates are evaluated against the ORIGINAL tree, the mask lands on a copy. Inert on any frame without the line; the #1039 parity test in `CaptureRedactionCorpusTest` pins that every rule declares it, so a new dropoff surface cannot forget.",
          "find": {
            "hasTextStartsWith": "Apt/Suite"
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Apt/Suite: ",
            "Apt/Suite "
          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1039 belt: the LABEL-anchored half of the subpremise mask — an `Apt/Suite` label node followed by its value, whatever spelling that value carries (`Unit 4202` shipped raw 2026-08-14). Both label spellings are enumerated because `hasPrecedingSiblingText` is an exact, un-trimmed equality; whole-node `plainMask` because a unit number is a bounded ~10^4 alphabet. Ordered ahead of every generic shape entry in this block: `maskNode` is first-match-wins, and a digit-leading value (`101 B`) would otherwise be claimed and HASHED by the id-less street shape. See `dropoff_handoff` for the full reasoning. Inert without the label; pinned by the #1039 parity test.",
          "find": {
            "any": [
              {
                "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Apt/Suite"
              },
              {
                "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Apt/Suite:"
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        }
      ],
      "state": {
        "flow": "post:task",
        "modeHint": "online"
      },
      "require": {
        "all": [
          {
            "allTextContainsAny": [
              "this offer",
              "delivery complete"
            ]
          },
          {
            "allTextContainsAll": [
              "doordash pay",
              "customer tips"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "reject": [
        {
          "allTextContains": "pause orders"
        }
      ],
      "parse": {
        "as": "post_task",
        "fields": {
          // #1029: the offer's own total. Arm 1 is the `final_value` id every
          // committed pre-8.93.7 fixture carries (byte-identical there). Arm 2 is
          // the 8.93.7 render, which ships the whole sheet WITHOUT money view ids:
          // the figure is a plain TextView a couple of slots after the 'This offer'
          // section title, with a Collapse/Expand affordance in between — hence the
          // shape-anchored scan rather than a positional `sibling(N)`. The pattern
          // IS the safety: only a well-formed currency node can be picked up, so a
          // re-ordered row reads null, never the wrong node. The pattern text is
          // byte-pinned to `CurrencyShape.RULE_PATTERN` (one shape definition shared
          // with `parseGlyphCurrency`) and the trailing `, 2` caps the scan at this
          // row's own width — see the tips field below for why an uncapped scan is
          // fail-WRONG on a row whose value is missing.
          "totalPay": {
            "coalesce": [
              {
                "find": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "final_value"
                },
                "read": "text",
                "transform": "parseCurrency"
              },
              {
                "find": {
                  "hasText": "This offer"
                },
                "navigate": "nextSiblingMatchingRegex(^\\$(?:0|[1-9]\\d{0,3}|[1-9],\\d{3})\\.\\d{2}$, 2)",
                "read": "text",
                "transform": "parseCurrency"
              }
            ]
          },
          // #1029: DELIBERATELY left as the exact-offset read, and deliberately NOT
          // given a currency scan. On 8.93.7 'DoorDash pay' is a HEADER, not a
          // label — its value is the sum of the 'Base pay'/'Peak pay' items that
          // follow it — so a `nextSiblingMatchingRegex` here would happily return
          // Base pay's $8.70 and call it the whole app-pay side. The offset read is
          // safe by accident of the alphabet: on 8.93.7 sibling(1) is the literal
          // text 'Base pay', which `parseCurrency` rejects, so the field is null on
          // that build — honest, and the `sumApproxEquals` validator below PASSES on
          // a null summand by design (see ValidateRegistry), so a null here cannot
          // drop the rest of the parse.
          "appPay": {
            "find": {
              "hasTextContaining": "DoorDash pay"
            },
            "navigate": "sibling(1)",
            "read": "text",
            "transform": "parseCurrency"
          },
          // #1029: this one FAILED WRONG, which is worse than failing null. 8.93.7
          // renders the breakdown as flat id-less siblings — 'Customer tips', '799',
          // '$7.00' — where '799' is a DoorDash type CODE (older builds render it in
          // the `pay_line_item_title` slot, which is why it reads as harmless there).
          // `sibling(1)` therefore handed `parseCurrency` the string '799' and the
          // parse reported a $799.00 tip on a $16.70 delivery. Scanning for the first
          // following sibling whose text IS a currency figure skips the code node and
          // removes the fabrication structurally: no offset can be right on both
          // layouts, but "the next money-shaped node" is right on both.
          // The `, 2` cap is the other half of that: if the tips VALUE is simply
          // absent (['Customer tips', '799', 'Peak pay', '$1.00']) an uncapped scan
          // walks out of the row and returns Peak pay's $1.00 as the tip — fail-WRONG,
          // and the `sumApproxEquals` validator cannot catch it because `appPay` is
          // null on 8.93.7. Two is the fielded distance (label -> optional type code
          // -> value).
          // RESIDUAL (documented, not built): the cap bounds DISTANCE, not row
          // OWNERSHIP. With the type-code node ABSENT as well — ['Customer tips',
          // 'Peak pay', '$1.00'] — a cap of 2 still reaches Peak pay's figure and
          // reports it as the tip. No fielded frame has that shape (8.93.7 renders
          // the tips row LAST and always with the code present), and tightening the
          // cap to 1 would break the fielded row it exists for. #1051 closes it
          // properly with the label-based `each` over the breakdown rows, which reads
          // each row's own value instead of scanning past its end.
          "customerTips": {
            "find": {
              "hasTextContaining": "Customer tips"
            },
            "navigate": "nextSiblingMatchingRegex(^\\$(?:0|[1-9]\\d{0,3}|[1-9],\\d{3})\\.\\d{2}$, 2)",
            "read": "text",
            "transform": "parseCurrency"
          },
          "payLineItems": {
            "each": {
              "hasIdSuffix": "pay_line_item_title"
            },
            "scope": "ancestor(1)",
            "extract": {
              "type": {
                "find": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "pay_line_item_title"
                },
                "read": "text"
              },
              "amount": {
                "find": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "pay_line_item_value"
                },
                "read": "text",
                "transform": "parseCurrency"
              }
            }
          },
          "isExpanded": {
            "literal": true
          },
          // #1029, superseding the #801/#819 "leave it null" stance: the "This dash so
          // far" session total. Arm 1 is the settled `earnings_ticker` TextView that
          // pre-0.230.0 builds carry (every corpus frame that HAS the id resolves here,
          // byte-identically). Arm 2 reads the id-less ANIMATED digit-wheel that
          // replaced it — the parent of the label node, whose `allText` fuses to
          // "This dash so far$16.70".
          // #801/#819 declined to read that wheel because "the digits do NOT reliably
          // reconstruct the value". True — and that is exactly the judgement
          // `parseGlyphCurrency` now makes FOR the rule: it keeps only the currency
          // glyphs and then FULL-matches a settled shape, so the mid-spin frames in this
          // corpus ("$016.603" on 07-17, "$70103.030" on the 8.93.7 collapsed capture)
          // still yield null, while every settled wheel parses — including the five
          // 2026-04-19 fixtures this rule has been silently dropping since 0.230.0.
          // The transform cannot catch a mid-spin read that is well-FORMED but wrong;
          // that is the settle gate's job, and this feed IS behind it — the state layer
          // treats `sessionEarnings` exactly like the idle pill's `sessionPay`, because
          // it is literally the same digit-wheel component.
          // The dedupeKey stays `{totalPay}` (the #427/#606/#801 dead-template lint).
          "sessionEarnings": {
            "coalesce": [
              {
                "find": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "earnings_ticker"
                },
                "read": "allText",
                "transform": "parseCurrency"
              },
              {
                "find": {
                  "hasText": "This dash so far"
                },
                "navigate": "parent",
                "read": "allText",
                "transform": "parseGlyphCurrency"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      },
      "validate": [
        {
          "assert": "sumApproxEquals",
          "fields": [
            "appPay",
            "customerTips"
          ],
          "target": "totalPay",
          "tolerance": 0.02,
          "onFail": "skip"
        }
      ],
      "effects": [
        {
          "screenshot": {
            "prefix": "DeliveryBreakdown - {totalPay}",
            "category": "delivery_summary"
          },
          "dedupeKey": "delivery-ss-expanded-{totalPay}",
          "throttleMs": 60000
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.delivery_summary_collapsed",
      "priority": 31,
      "redact": [
        {
          "comment": "#993 belt (the FIX 3 precedent): this dropoff-phase surface can win a frame that STILL carries DoorDash's nav arrival banner — `dropoff_navigation`'s own reject (Continue / Complete Delivery / Mark as delivered / complete_delivery_steps_button) hands the arrival-MOMENT frame to whichever arrived-phase rule matches, and nav_arriving rejects bottom-sheet frames, so neither of the two rules that already masked `arriving_at_title` covers it. The node is id-BEARING, so the id-less name/address entries elsewhere in this block structurally cannot reach it (`hasNoId` conjunct). Whole token, no normalize — it is a customer ADDRESS on every dropoff-phase surface (see dropoff_navigation for the full reasoning). Inert on any frame without the node; `DropoffBannerRedactParityTest` pins that EVERY rule in this file carries it, so a new dropoff surface cannot forget.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "arriving_at_title"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#910 V1 — this rule shipped with NO redact block and it is the HIGHEST-severity site of the 07-29 pull: 4 of the 8 fielded `delivery_summary_collapsed` envelopes are not the pay-breakdown card at all but the DROP-OFF card (the require is a text-only `allTextContainsAny:['this offer','delivery complete']`, which a live drop-off sheet satisfies), so they carry the full customer block — `user_name`, `address_line_1` (house number + street), `address_line_2` (city/ST/ZIP), the dasher instruction body and the customer's per-item note — all raw on a RECOGNIZED envelope. `CustomerTextMarkers` cannot own any of it: DoorDash renders this block as SPLIT nodes (the 'Delivery for' lead-in is its own `user_name_label` sibling), so the name/address nodes are BARE and a text-prefix scan is structurally blind to them. The entries below are the `dropoff_pre_arrival` (priority 73) id set — the same ids, the same masks — so one customer redacts to one hex everywhere. Capture-only: this rule parses none of these nodes, so the parse goldens are byte-identical. Genuine summary frames (the committed corpus) carry none of these ids, so the block is inert there. Deliberately NOT redacted: `order_contents_header_title` (the merchant — driver-owned, not PII), `order_item_name` (catalog item), and every pay node.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "user_name"
          },
          "normalize": "customerName"
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "address_line_1"
          }
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "address_line_2"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "The Apt/Suite VALUE, keeping its app-vocabulary label prefix exactly as `dropoff_pre_arrival` does. #934: and, exactly as that sibling, `plainMask` — this is the #910-added mirror the issue names, and a mirror must inherit the family's posture or the fix has a hole precisely where the surface was copied. Bounded unit number after the label strip; distinctness redundant beside this rule's own `user_name`/`address_line_*` hashes.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "address_subpremise_line"
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Apt/Suite: ",
            "Apt/Suite "
          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1058 review (Astra xhigh) \u2014 `plainMask`, the #920/#985 posture. The node is the customer's own delivery-instruction body, and its ALPHABET is unbounded but its LENGTH is not: a SHORT instruction that IS the secret (\"Hand it to me: 4417\") masks whole to `[redacted:<4hex>]`, and enumerating `Hand it to me: 0000`\u2026`9999` against those 65 536 buckets recovers the code \u2014 the #889 F1 bounded-secret argument, arriving through length rather than through alphabet. The distinctness the hash buys is worth nothing here to trade against that: unlike a customer NAME (which `normalize: customerName` turns into a cross-surface join key) an instruction body joins nothing and is never parsed. Pre-existing on this rule; #1058's fourth `require` arm is what EXPOSED it, by making the alcohol render \u2014 whose instruction body carried a door code \u2014 a recognized frame this entry actually fires on. Both states of the field carry the same declaration, and `plainMask` is legal here precisely because there is no `normalize` (the compiler rejects the pair).",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "dasher_instruction_content_collapsed"
          },
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1058 review: `plainMask`, for the reason spelled out on the collapsed twin directly above \u2014 same customer text, same bounded-length secret, same #920/#985 posture. Enumerating only the state that happened to field is the debt #986 already paid for once.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "dasher_instruction_content_expanded"
          },
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "The customer's free-text note on an order item — the #803 class (a note routinely carries a gate code, an apartment number, or a callback instruction). Masked whole; the item NAME node (`order_item_name`) is catalog data and stays raw.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "order_item_instructions"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1039 belt (the #993 combined-frame precedent): the FUSED subpremise line — `Apt/Suite <n>` or `Apt/Suite: <n>` in ONE id-less node, the render the committed `dropoff_pre_arrival` fixtures carry. Declared on EVERY rule in this file (plus `navigation_generic`) rather than only on the surfaces that fielded it, because a redact protects only the frames its OWN rule wins and the rules most likely to claim an address-block frame OUT-RANK the fielded pair (`delivery_summary_collapsed` 31, `dropoff_navigation` 61, `dropoff_photo` 62, `dropoff_geofence_warning` 84). Entry is `dropoff_photo`'s #986 entry verbatim — see it for the full reasoning: the token after the label strip is a bounded ~10^4 unit number, so a 4-hex distinctness suffix is brute-recoverable (`plainMask`), while the label itself is app vocabulary and is kept for replay context. ACCEPTED COLLATERAL: the split row's BARE `Apt/Suite` LABEL node is itself an exact prefix match here, and no keepPrefix can be kept from it, so it masks whole to plain `[redacted]` — two words of app chrome lost on a debug envelope, the same fail-toward-privacy trade `DropoffBannerRedactParityTest` already documents for the `Arriving at` label. It cannot break the label-sibling entry below: redact predicates are evaluated against the ORIGINAL tree, the mask lands on a copy. Inert on any frame without the line; the #1039 parity test in `CaptureRedactionCorpusTest` pins that every rule declares it, so a new dropoff surface cannot forget.",
          "find": {
            "hasTextStartsWith": "Apt/Suite"
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Apt/Suite: ",
            "Apt/Suite "
          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1039 belt: the LABEL-anchored half of the subpremise mask — an `Apt/Suite` label node followed by its value, whatever spelling that value carries (`Unit 4202` shipped raw 2026-08-14). Both label spellings are enumerated because `hasPrecedingSiblingText` is an exact, un-trimmed equality; whole-node `plainMask` because a unit number is a bounded ~10^4 alphabet. Ordered ahead of every generic shape entry in this block: `maskNode` is first-match-wins, and a digit-leading value (`101 B`) would otherwise be claimed and HASHED by the id-less street shape. See `dropoff_handoff` for the full reasoning. Inert without the label; pinned by the #1039 parity test.",
          "find": {
            "any": [
              {
                "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Apt/Suite"
              },
              {
                "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Apt/Suite:"
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        }
      ],
      "state": {
        "flow": "post:task",
        "modeHint": "online"
      },
      "require": {
        "allTextContainsAny": [
          "this offer",
          "delivery complete"
        ]
      },
      "reject": [
        {
          "allTextContains": "pause orders"
        },
        {
          "allTextContains": "Pickup from"
        },
        {
          "allTextContains": "Arrived at store"
        },
        {
          "allTextContains": "Start pickup"
        },
        {
          "allTextContains": "Total will be higher"
        },
        {
          "allTextContains": "High paying offer"
        },
        {
          "allTextContains": "Additional"
        }
      ],
      "comment": "#564: reject the OFFER-only markers above ('Total will be higher'/'High paying offer'/'Additional') — an add-on offer popup carries 'this offer' (in 'priority for this offer') which satisfies require, but it is NOT a delivery summary. Without these rejects a transient offer/loading frame mid-stack masqueraded as a collapsed summary and fabricated a $0 completion (06-21 seq98, Burger King add-on).",
      "parse": {
        "as": "post_task",
        "fields": {
          // #1029 — the expanded rule's arm pair, mirrored (see it for the reasoning):
          // `final_value` first (every corpus frame that has the id resolves there),
          // then the 8.93.7 id-less render, where the figure is a currency-shaped
          // TextView a couple of slots after the 'This offer' section title with the
          // Expand affordance in between. Same byte-pinned `CurrencyShape` pattern and
          // same `, 2` row cap as the expanded rule.
          "totalPay": {
            "coalesce": [
              {
                "find": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "final_value"
                },
                "read": "text",
                "transform": "parseCurrency"
              },
              {
                "find": {
                  "hasText": "This offer"
                },
                "navigate": "nextSiblingMatchingRegex(^\\$(?:0|[1-9]\\d{0,3}|[1-9],\\d{3})\\.\\d{2}$, 2)",
                "read": "text",
                "transform": "parseCurrency"
              }
            ]
          },
          "isExpanded": {
            "literal": false
          },
          "expandButtonId": {
            "coalesce": [
              {
                "find": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "expandable_view"
                },
                "read": "viewIdResourceName"
              },
              {
                "find": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "expandable_layout"
                },
                "read": "viewIdResourceName"
              }
            ]
          },
          // #1029 — the expanded rule's `sessionEarnings` arm pair, mirrored; see it for
          // the full reasoning. #801 was right that the raw digit-wheel "does NOT reliably
          // reconstruct the value" — the two mid-spin frames it names ("$0.00", "$016.603")
          // are in THIS folder — so the reliability judgement now lives in
          // `parseGlyphCurrency`, which full-matches a settled shape and returns null on
          // both of them, rather than in a decision to not look at all. The 8.93.7
          // collapsed capture is a third such frame ("$70103.030" → null); its sibling
          // expanded capture, taken 4 s later, has settled and parses $16.70.
          // The dedupeKey stays `{totalPay}` (the #427/#606 dead-template class).
          "sessionEarnings": {
            "coalesce": [
              {
                "find": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "earnings_ticker"
                },
                "read": "allText",
                "transform": "parseCurrency"
              },
              {
                "find": {
                  "hasText": "This dash so far"
                },
                "navigate": "parent",
                "read": "allText",
                "transform": "parseGlyphCurrency"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      },
      "bind": {
        // #734: bind ONLY the per-delivery pay-breakdown expandable ('This offer' /
        // 'DoorDash pay'), never the dash-stats expandable. The collapsed summary renders
        // TWO nodes sharing the expandable_view id — the online-time/stats section AND the
        // pay section — so the old `any:[expandable_view, expandable_layout]` bound the
        // first-in-tree (stats) node, and at fire time the app-owned actuation ranker saw a
        // 2-candidate tie (both share the id, EXPAND_EARNINGS is label-free) and clicked the
        // first. Anchor on the stats section's stable 'Total online time' row (present in
        // every frame) and NEGATE it: the pay expandable never carries that text, so this
        // selects exactly the pay node. Only the pay expandable_view is clickable in the
        // field; clickNodeStrict taps it directly. A residual fire-time tie (shared id) now
        // aborts to manual (#425 fail-closed).
        "expandButton": {
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasIdSuffix": "expandable_view"
              },
              {
                "not": {
                  "hasAnyText": "Total online time"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          "optional": true
        }
      },
      "effects": [
        {
          "screenshot": {
            "prefix": "Delivery - {totalPay}",
            "category": "delivery_summary"
          },
          "dedupeKey": "delivery-ss-collapsed-{totalPay}",
          "throttleMs": 60000
        }
      ]
    },
    {
